# Netlify reads this file automatically on every deploy (including a
# manual zip upload) and applies these response headers.
#
# Why this exists: every round of fixes gets deployed as a fresh zip, but
# without this, browsers and Netlify's CDN are free to keep serving an
# OLD cached copy of a page for a while after a new one goes live — so a
# member can click something and see old, already-fixed behavior simply
# because their browser hasn't fetched the new version yet. This forces
# every page load to check with the server first (a fast "still current?"
# check, not a full re-download when nothing changed), so everyone always
# gets what's actually live.
/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Oct 2026 (speed pass): files that never change under the same name are saved
# by the phone/browser for a long time instead of being re-checked on every page
# load. Only these three groups are listed — pages, CSS and the app's own JS stay
# on the "always check" rule above so fixes still show up right away.
# If you ever replace one of these files, give the new one a new name (or ask
# Claude to bump it), because browsers will keep the old copy until it expires.
# Speed Check (speedcheck.html) has a row that shows which rule is really live.
/assets/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
/js/vendor/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
/assets/*.jpg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
/assets/*.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
